{
  "mn138:0.1": "Middle Discourses 138 ",
  "mn138:0.2": "A Summary Recital and its Analysis ",
  "mn138:1.1": "So I have heard. ",
  "mn138:1.2": "At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. ",
  "mn138:1.3": "There the Buddha addressed the mendicants, ",
  "mn138:1.4": "“Mendicants!” ",
  "mn138:1.5": "“Venerable sir,” they replied. ",
  "mn138:1.6": "The Buddha said this: ",
  "mn138:2.1": "“Mendicants, I shall teach you a summary recital and its analysis. ",
  "mn138:2.2": "Listen and apply your mind well, I will speak.” ",
  "mn138:2.3": "“Yes, sir,” they replied. ",
  "mn138:2.4": "The Buddha said this: ",
  "mn138:3.1": "“A mendicant should examine in whatever way such that, while examining, consciousness would neither be scattered nor diffused externally, nor stuck internally, and they would not be anxious because of grasping. ",
  "mn138:3.2": "When consciousness is neither scattered nor diffused externally, nor stuck internally, and they are not anxious because of grasping, there is no coming to be of the origin of suffering—of rebirth, old age, and death in the future.” ",
  "mn138:4.1": "That is what the Buddha said. ",
  "mn138:4.2": "When he had spoken, the Holy One rose from his seat and entered his dwelling. ",
  "mn138:5.1": "Soon after the Buddha left, those mendicants considered, ",
  "mn138:5.2": "“The Buddha gave this brief summary recital, then entered his dwelling without explaining the meaning in detail.